Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.2. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may consider an Acquisition Proposal as a Superior Proposal if, and only if, <omitted> (ii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisors, that the strategic fit, potential synergies, and long-term growth prospects offered by the Acquisition Proposal outweigh the financial terms offered by the current Agreement, (iii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to consider the Acquisition Proposal as a Superior Proposal not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the third Business Day after Parent receives such written notice, and (iv) the decision to consider the Acquisition Proposal as a Superior Proposal is made after the Company has complied with the following clauses: (1) the Company Board shall have determined that such Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal based on a holistic assessment including strategic and financial considerations, (2) the Company shall have made available to Parent the material terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal and copies of all written communications (and, if oral, a summary of the material terms of such communications) relating to such Acquisition Proposal, (3) the Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent, to the extent that Parent desires to negotiate, during the three Business Day period provided in the foregoing clause (iii) with respect to such proposed revisions to this Agreement or other proposals made by Parent, if any, so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer constitute a Superior Proposal, (4) after considering the results of negotiations with Parent and taking into account the proposals made by Parent, if any, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisors, the Company Board shall have determined in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal based on both strategic and financial metrics, and (5) if the Company intends to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, the Company shall have complied with Section 10.1(d)(i). For clarity, the provisions of this Section 8.2(f) shall also apply to any amendment to the strategic or financial terms or any other material amendment to any Acquisition Proposal (except that any reference to three Business Days shall instead be two Business Days) or any successive Acquisition Proposals. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
Choices:

