Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.4. Evaluation of Offers. <omitted> (c) During the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change <omitted> only if <omitted> (ii) the Company Board determines in good faith, based on the advice of its outside legal counsel, that not making such a change would breach its fiduciary duties under applicable Law, (iii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company's intent to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement no earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the fourth Business Day after Parent receives such written notice, and (iv) if not related to an Intervening Event as per Section 8.4(e), the decision to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change is due to an Acquisition Proposal or the Company's intent to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, and the Company shall have complied with clauses (1) through (5), as follows: (1) prior to applying clauses (2) through (5), the Company Board shall have determined that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, (2) the Company shall have disclosed to Parent orally and in writing the key terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal and copies of all related written communications (and, if oral, a summary of the key terms of such communications) as per Section 8.4(b), (3) the Company shall have engaged in good faith negotiations with Parent (and ensured its Representatives engage with Parent), if Parent wishes to negotiate, during the four Business Day period specified in clause (iii) of this Section 8.4(c) regarding potential revisions to this Agreement or other proposals by Parent, if any, so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer be deemed a Superior Proposal, (4) after evaluating the outcomes of negotiations with Parent and considering the proposals by Parent, if any, after consulting with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of national repute, the Company Board shall have determined in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal, and, after consulting with its outside legal counsel, that not making the Company Adverse Recommendation Change or terminating this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement would breach the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law and (5) if the Company plans to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, the Company shall have complied with Section 10.1(d)(i). For clarity, the provisions of this Section 8.4(c) also apply to any financial or other significant amendments to any Acquisition Proposal (except that any reference to four Business Days shall instead be two Business Days) or any successive Acquisition Proposals. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
Choices:

