{question}

{choices}

Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 6.3. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may change its recommendation if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, that the failure to make such a change would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law, (ii) the Company has given Parent written notice of its intention to change its recommendation not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the third Business Day after Parent receives such written notice, (iii) the Company Board has determined that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal after considering the financial aspects and the regulatory compliance required, and (iv) the Company Board has considered all relevant factors, including potential impacts on employees and community stakeholders, and determined that the financial point of view is the most significant but not the sole consideration. For clarity, any amendment to the financial terms or any other material amendment to any Acquisition Proposal shall require a new notice period of two Business Days. (Pages 45-46)\nAnswer:
Choices:
